General description:

AMG is a parallel algebraic multigrid solver for linear systems arising from
problems on unstructured grids.  The driver provided with AMG builds linear 
systems for various 3-dimensional problems.
AMG is written in ISO-C.  It is an SPMD code which uses MPI and OpenMP 
threading within MPI tasks. Parallelism is achieved by data decomposition. The 
driver provided with AMG achieves this decomposition by simply subdividing 
driver provided with AMG achieves this decomposition by simply subdividing 
the grid into logical P x Q x R (in 3D) chunks of equal size.
For more information, see the amg.readme file in the docs directory of the
distribution.
%==========================================================================
%==========================================================================

Building the Code

AMG is built as part of the AURORA CMake test suite (requires MPI):

    $ cmake -DBUILD_TESTING=ON ...
    $ cmake --build <build-dir> --target amg amg_aurora amg_veloc

Three executables are produced:

  amg        - no checkpointing
  amg_aurora - AURORA checkpoint backend
  amg_veloc  - VELOC checkpoint backend (only built when VELOC is found)

Usage:

    mpirun -np <x> ./amg -problem 2 -n nx ny nz -P Q R [-cp <interval>]
                          [-ckpt <path>] [-ckpt-host <host>]

Problem 2 runs GMRES over multiple time steps and is the checkpoint/restart
benchmark. Checkpointing is controlled by the checkpoint interval:

  -cp <interval>   checkpoint frequency in time steps
                   (0 disables checkpoints)

A state checkpoint is written at the start of every interval-th time step.

AURORA requires a checkpoint directory and optionally a backend hostname:

    mpirun -np <x> ./amg_aurora -problem 2 -n nx ny nz -P Q R -cp <interval> \
        -ckpt <checkpoint-dir> [-ckpt-host <hostname>]

VELOC requires a VELOC configuration file:

    mpirun -np <x> ./amg_veloc -problem 2 -n nx ny nz -P Q R -cp <interval> \
        -ckpt <veloc-config.cfg>

Restart is automatic: if a checkpoint is available, the application restores
the latest version and continues from that time step. Delete the
contents of the checkpoint directory before a fresh run.

%==========================================================================
%==========================================================================

Figure of Merit (FOM)

For problem 1, 
there are 2 FOMs printed out at the end of each run:
nnz_AP / setup_time
nnz_AP * #iterations / solve time
Both need to be considered.

For problem 2,
one FOM needs to be considered:
nnz_AP * (#iterations + time_steps) / time

